🔹 1️⃣ Creating a File

Using File class:

import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;

public class CreateFileExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        File file = new File("sample.txt");

        if (file.createNewFile()) {
            System.out.println("File Created");
        } else {
            System.out.println("File already exists");
        }
    }
}

🔹 2️⃣ Writing to a File
✅ Using FileWriter
import java.io.FileWriter;
import java.io.IOException;

public class WriteExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        FileWriter writer = new FileWriter("sample.txt");
        writer.write("Hello Vaishnavi");
        writer.close();
    }
}


👉 Overwrites existing content.

✅ Append Mode
FileWriter writer = new FileWriter("sample.txt", true);

🔹 3️⃣ Reading from a File
✅ Using Scanner
import java.io.File;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class ReadExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        File file = new File("sample.txt");
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(file);

        while (sc.hasNextLine()) {
            System.out.println(sc.nextLine());
        }
        sc.close();
    }
}

🔹 4️⃣ Using BufferedReader (Better Performance)
import java.io.*;

public class BufferedReadExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("sample.txt"));

        String line;
        while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
            System.out.println(line);
        }

        br.close();
    }
}

🔹 5️⃣ Using BufferedWriter
BufferedWriter bw = new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter("sample.txt"));
bw.write("Java File Handling");
bw.close();


👉 Faster than FileWriter.

🔹 6️⃣ Deleting a File
File file = new File("sample.txt");

if (file.delete()) {
    System.out.println("Deleted Successfully");
}

🔹 7️⃣ Modern Way (Java 7+) – NIO
Writing
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.io.IOException;

public class NIOWrite {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        Files.write(Paths.get("sample.txt"), "Hello".getBytes());
    }
}

Reading
Files.readAllLines(Paths.get("sample.txt"))
     .forEach(System.out::println);

🔹 Important Interview Points

✔ Always close streams (or use try-with-resources)
✔ Buffered streams are faster
✔ File class handles file metadata
✔ IOException is checked exception
✔ NIO is preferred in modern applications

🔥 Best Practice (Try-With-Resources)
try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("sample.txt"))) {
    System.out.println(br.readLine());
}


👉 Automatically closes resource.

